Comment (pkg/agentconfig/sensitive.go:174):
**High** · security/perf

`hasURLPassword` is quadratic. For every `scheme://` match it parses the whole rest of the string (`candidate := s[loc[0]:]`), only cut at whitespace or quotes. A value like `"a://a://a://…"` has no terminator, so the work is (n/4 matches) × n.

Measured with `containsSecretValue`: 8 KiB → 65 ms, 16 KiB → 180 ms, 32 KiB → 710 ms (4× per 2× input). Three paths reach it:
- `PUT /agent/instances/:id/config-report` → `RedactDocument` (4 MiB body; projected hours of CPU per request, any agent credential);
- `POST …/config/preview` → `Redact(eff)` per instance (only needs `agent:read`);
- the agent's own `Redact`/`Digest`, if such a value is saved in an overlay.

Suggested fix: end each candidate at the next scheme match (or cap it at ~2 KiB) before `url.Parse`, and cap the number of candidates / input length that `containsSecretValue` inspects. Add a regression test that runs a 1 MiB `a://` string under a time bound.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Fix in pkg/agentconfig/sensitive.go hasURLPassword: bound each candidate so total work is linear. For each scheme match at loc[0], end the candidate at the earliest of: the next scheme match start (the next element of the FindAllStringIndex result), the first URL terminator, and loc[0]+2048 bytes. Also cap the number of scheme matches inspected (e.g. 64) and the input length scanned for URL passwords (e.g. first 64 KiB; the token regexes are RE2/linear, keep them on the full string). Regression test: a 1 MiB strings.Repeat("a://", n) completes under a generous bound (e.g. 2s) and a real https://u:pw@h within the cap is still detected. Keep Redact/Digest/RedactDocument rules identical otherwise.

