Comment (internal/api/handler/agent_config.go:865):
**Medium** · security

`revisionResponse` returns `rev.Overlay` verbatim from `GET …/config` and `GET …/config/revisions/{rev}`, both guarded by `agent:read`. Under Cedar, viewer, auditor and contributor all hold read. A literal secret typed into `plugins.*.config` is readable by every viewer, in every revision, forever (revisions can't be deleted; see the thread on `BeforeDelete`).

I know this is documented (R57), but docs won't stop operators pasting a DB password, which is the normal pattern in agent config files. Suggestion, either:
- reject literal secret-like values at write time in `ValidateOverlay` (reuse `isSecretKey`/`containsSecretValue`) unless the value is placeholder-only, which forces `${env:NAME}`; or
- return `Redact`-ed overlays to callers without `agent:configure`.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Owner decision (supersedes R57): redact overlays for readers without agent:configure. In internal/api/handler/agent_config.go Get and GetRevision, decide whether the caller holds agent:configure on this agent by evaluating the PDP for the request subject (inject what you need into AgentConfigHandler from api.go RegisterHandlers, e.g. the *middleware.PEP or the authz PDP; look for an existing in-handler permission-check helper before adding one). If not allowed, or the evaluation errors / PDP unavailable (fail closed), pass the overlay through agentconfig.RedactDocument before returning. Editors keep the verbatim overlay. Update the swag @Description (drop the "returned unredacted to every agent:read holder" wording), docs mentioning R57, and the PR body. Integration tests: an agent:read-only caller gets ••••; a configure holder gets the literal. Do not change PUT/revert.

