Comment (internal/service/relational/agentcfg/service.go:409):
**Medium** · bug

The cap counts every row that isn't prune-eligible, so a daemon instance that stopped reporting minutes ago still counts for 30 days (`InstanceRetention`). Instance IDs change on restart (e.g. emptyDir state), so a 50-pod DaemonSet restarted 10 times in a month hits 500. New pods then get 409 on report and their heartbeats are silently not registered; a leaked key can also fill the cap on purpose. There's no per-instance delete, only deleting the agent.

Suggested fix: count only fresh (non-stale) instances against the cap, or evict the oldest stale row when inserting a new one. An admin route to delete one instance would also help.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Count only fresh instances toward the cap. In agentcfg/service.go countActiveInstances, count rows that are NOT stale by the same rule IsStale uses (mirror its threshold and daemon handling in SQL, keep NULL-safe comparisons). Update the doc comment (R37 wording) and any docs/.env.example text about the cap. Service integration test: stale daemon rows do not block a new instance at the cap; fresh rows do. Do not change pruning.

