Comment (pkg/agentconfig/classify.go:199):
**Medium** · security (design question)

`enabled`, `policy_data`, `labels`, `policy_behavior` and `schedule` are all classified `data-only` (Safe), plugin removal is `reduces-scope` (Safe), and `usedSources` includes disabled plugins. So under `apply_safe`:
- a remote admin can re-enable a plugin the host operator disabled on purpose (including a local binary), and it will run;
- a remote admin can change the policy inputs that decide pass/fail, or remove the plugin that collects failing evidence, without the host owner opting in.

Is this intended? Suggestion: classify `enabled` false→true as Unsafe (like a new source unless trusted), and consider making `policy_data`, `policy_behavior` and plugin removal Unsafe or opt-in via something like `overridable_config_flags`.
Replies:
**lisa implementation guidance:** Owner decision: narrow to re-enabling only. In pkg/agentconfig/classify.go, when a plugin is disabled in the base (bp present and !bp.IsEnabled()) and enabled in eff, emit Change{Path: /plugins/<p>/enabled, Safety: Unsafe, Reason: new ChangeReasonReenablePlugin = "reenables-plugin"} unless MatchTrustedSource(rc, ep.Source), in which case Safe with ChangeReasonTrustedSource. Disabling stays Safe. policy_data/policy_behavior/labels/schedule and plugin removal stay Safe by design: say so in the Classify doc comment and docs. Add the reason wherever reason vocabularies are listed, tests, and regenerate swagger if needed.

